---
title: "FrontierJS — the tutorial is a command"
description: "fli tutor is thirteen lessons that run the real commands and then ask the running world whether they worked — including a real deploy to your own machine. It is graded by the project's own CI."
render: static
---
<!--
  The tutorial page.

  Its whole argument is that a tutorial which cannot fail is not evidence, so
  every sample here is a transcript or a verbatim lift from the step's own
  source. The ten lesson cards are markup; there is nothing on this page for
  an island to move.
-->
<script>
  export let data = null

  const sample = data?.samples ?? {}

  // The lessons, as they are on disk. Step counts are file counts in
  // packages/cli/commands/tutor/_steps-*/ — a number that goes stale by
  // somebody deleting a step, which is the kind that shows.
  const lessons = [
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:app',
      name:  'An app that runs',
      steps: 11,
      needs: 'nothing',
      body:  'Scaffold an app, start it, register an account, add a model of your own, push it, write a row through the API and read it back out of the SQLite file the app wrote. Then write a Mesa component, and ask the dev server for the compiled module. Then build it, and check the built page actually loads its JavaScript.',
      proves: 'the row is in db/app.db',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:tools',
      name:  'The four tools, and when',
      steps: 6,
      needs: 'nothing',
      body:  'Four surfaces whose whole job is to report on a running app, and one question each. The GUI is the front door — what is here, what is up, and does anything fail a check. Then the database as it really is, the call feed with a refusal in it named and coded, and the chain that handled the request.',
      proves: 'a 401 shows up as a 401',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:ui',
      name:  'A screen you did not write',
      steps: 6,
      needs: 'Chrome',
      body:  'A form is not written, it is read off the schema — which is easy to nod at and easy to fake, so this one opens a real browser. Every control, the type each column implies, and nothing for the columns the server owns. Then one attribute is added to one column of the schema, no form is touched, and the same page starts refusing.',
      proves: 'the empty submit never reaches the server',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:access',
      name:  'Who may do what',
      steps: 9,
      needs: 'nothing',
      body:  'Authorization is declared in the schema rather than written in hooks, which is easy to nod at and hard to believe. So this one changes one line of db/schema.lite at a time and shows the answer to the same HTTP request changing: a gate that refuses, a policy that filters, a column that silently declines a write.',
      proves: 'the same request answers 200, then 401',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:live',
      name:  'A change reaching somebody else',
      steps: 8,
      needs: 'nothing',
      body:  'A row is written over plain HTTP and a socket that asked for nothing is told about it. Then the second question, which most frameworks never ask: who is on the other end? Two sockets, one signed in and one not, watched against a single publish before and after the gate goes up.',
      proves: 'the anonymous one is not told',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:jobs',
      name:  'Work that outlives the request',
      steps: 6,
      needs: 'nothing',
      body:  'A queue that is a SQLite file, a job named by its own filename, and a dispatch from a service hook. What is asserted is the order — the response comes back with the work not yet done, and the row changes afterwards. A test that only checked the final state would pass with the work done synchronously.',
      proves: 'the response came back before the work was done',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:notify',
      name:  'Telling somebody something',
      steps: 8,
      needs: 'nothing',
      body:  'One notification, declared in one file, delivered as a row in the app and as an email — and the file names it, so the type nothing states is the type the database carries. Renaming the file renames the type and orphans every row already written, in silence, which is why a stated type exists. Then a transport declared with no formatter, refused before anything is delivered.',
      proves: 'a two-transport notification cannot half-land',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:site',
      name:  'The public half',
      steps: 8,
      needs: 'nothing',
      body:  'A prerendered site is a peer surface, not a folder in the SPA. One HTML file per page with the data already in it — and a build that refuses to emit a page whose load() read something gated, because a published page cannot be recalled. You watch the refusal, then the override that gets past it.',
      proves: 'the build refuses to publish a gated model',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:deploy',
      name:  'A deploy you can take back',
      steps: 10,
      needs: 'Docker · git',
      body:  'A deploy target may be localhost, so this is the real pipeline: an image, a journal on disk, a health poll, a swap. Deploy, change a line and deploy again, then revert — and revert the revert. Asserted against the image id the container is on, because a pipeline that reports success while leaving the old container up passes everything else.',
      proves: 'the previous release is serving again',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:change',
      name:  'The schema you already deployed',
      steps: 6,
      needs: 'nothing',
      body:  'Month two: rows in production and a release already serving them. Three changes, each classified — an optional column you can take back, a required one that makes the deploy a pivot and comes back with the three-deploy split, and a raised gate that touches no column and is still a contract.',
      proves: 'a gate raise is a contract, with no column touched',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:test',
      name:  'Knowing it is right',
      steps: 6,
      needs: 'nothing',
      body:  'Most of what you would write tests for has already been declared, and a declaration can be executed: four calls run every gate at every level, every protected column, every validator and every row policy, with no fixtures to author. Then the schema is mutated on purpose — a gate dropped, a guard removed — and the checks derived from the ORIGINAL are run against the mutant, so anything that survives is a hole in the checks and names itself.',
      proves: 'a survivor is a fact about the checks, not the schema',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:fleet',
      name:  'A machine that takes orders',
      steps: 8,
      needs: 'a checkout · Docker',
      body:  'The other release story. A control plane holds the fleet as rows, a machine runs an outpost so the control plane has hands on it, and nobody types a deploy. A signed command from the control plane really runs on your machine — and then a release: sources built into an image there, a container started, and the bytes recorded, because a tag is a name two builds share.',
      proves: 'the control plane recorded which bytes ran',
    },
    {
      cmd:   'fli tutor:adopt',
      name:  'A database you already have',
      steps: 7,
      needs: 'nothing',
      body:  'The other door. A SQLite database made by somebody else, with rows in it, read into a schema — and the half a converter can quietly skip: every construct the reading could not carry, graded changed, lost or noted. It checks the reading against itself by building a database from it and reading that, then serves a row written by raw SQL before there was an app. The last step is the finding an inherited schema comes with, and it is kept rather than fixed: a baseline the number may never pass.',
      proves: 'a row that predates the framework is served',
    },
  ]

  // The defects the tutorial found on the way to being written. Every one of
  // them exited 0, which is the argument for probing rather than trusting a
  // status code — a tutorial graded on exit codes teaches all six.
  const found = [
    { id: 'FJS-735', what: 'Nine of ten refusals in the scaffolder exited 0, so every wrapper around it read “scaffolded”.' },
    { id: 'FJS-736', what: 'The auth commands had rotted through five layers, ending with one that created an account and reported failure.' },
    { id: 'FJS-737', what: 'Every app scaffolded with auth booted with its policy claims graded against nothing — and said so on every boot, to nobody.' },
    { id: 'FJS-738', what: 'Every command the deploy pipeline sent to a machine did nothing at all under node, and reported success.' },
    { id: 'FJS-752', what: 'Every scaffolded app announced its writes on a channel no connection had joined — real-time wired everywhere, delivered nowhere.' },
    { id: 'FJS-758', what: 'Every file the static-site scaffold generated was broken, four different ways, because nothing had ever executed one.' },
  ]
</script>

<nav class="subnav" aria-label="Sections">
  <div class="container">
    <a href="#top">The idea</a>
    <a href="#step">A step</a>
    <a href="#lessons">The thirteen lessons</a>
