    <a href="#found">What it found</a>
    <a href="#start">Start</a>
  </div>
</nav>

<!-- ─── Hero ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="top">
  <div class="container hero">
    <div class="stack">
      <p class="eyebrow">The tutorial</p>
      <h1>The tutorial<br>is a command.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Not a page you read with a terminal open beside it. <code>fli tutor</code> prints
        its own prose, runs the real commands, and then <strong>asks the running world
        whether they worked</strong> — a port that answers, a table in
        <code>sqlite_master</code>, a row read back out of the file the app wrote.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        A step that cannot prove itself stops and tells you what it asked for and what it
        got instead. And the whole thing runs in this project's CI, so a command renamed
        out from under a step is a red build rather than a stale paragraph.
      </p>
      <div class="cluster">
        <a class="btn primary raised" href="#start">Run it</a>
        <a class="btn outlined" href="#lessons">The thirteen lessons</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <pre class="code">{@html sample.START}</pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── A step ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="step">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">A step</p>
    <h2>Every line beginning <span class="tick">✓</span> is a question that was asked.</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:64ch">
      This is lesson 1 applying a model you just wrote and then using it. Nothing here is
      an echo of a command that exited 0.
    </p>

    <div class="pair">
      <div>
        <p class="cap">What you see</p>
        <pre class="code">{@html sample.STEP}</pre>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="cap">What produced it</p>
        <pre class="code">{@html sample.ASSERT}</pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note">
      <b>Two things worth noticing.</b> The row is read out of the app's own SQLite file
      rather than off the response, because a service that answers 201 and writes nowhere
      passes every check above that line. And the refusal is a <b>pair</b> with the
      acceptance — the identical request without a token — because a refusal that cannot
      be shown to come from the rule it names proves nothing.
    </div>

    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:var(--space-xl)">And when it does not work</p>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.FAIL}</pre>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:64ch">
      The exit code is non-zero, nothing after the failing step ran, and the two ways
      forward are printed. Running the lesson again picks up where it stopped;
      <code>--step 1</code> runs that one step on its own.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── The lessons ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="lessons">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">The thirteen lessons</p>
    <h2>Empty directory to a machine taking orders.</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:64ch">
      Each one is separately runnable and separately resumable. The first two need nothing
      but Bun — no Docker, no network, no account, no browser tab open on somebody else's
      site.
    </p>

    <div class="lessons">
      {#each lessons as l}
        <article class="card lesson">
          <div class="split">
            <code class="cmd">{l.cmd}</code>
            <span class="badge muted">{l.steps} steps</span>
          </div>
          <h3>{l.name}</h3>
          <p>{l.body}</p>
          <div class="foot">
            <span class="pill">needs {l.needs}</span>
            <span class="proof"><span class="tick">✓</span> {l.proves}</span>
          </div>
        </article>
      {/each}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── Why ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="why">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why it is different</p>
    <h2>Three claims, and we have not found the third anywhere else.</h2>

    <div class="tiles">
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>You cannot fake a step</h3>
        <p>
          Progression is gated on a probe, the way rustlings gates on the compiler
          accepting your code. What is different is what the probe asks: not
          <em>did my command exit 0</em> but <em>is the row there</em>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>It teaches deployment</h3>
        <p>
          Most tutorials stop at localhost, because they cannot give you a server. This one
          does not need to: a deploy target may <em>be</em> <code>localhost</code>, so
          lesson 3 is a real image, a real journal on disk, a real swap and a real revert —
          on your own machine, and reversible.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>Our CI grades it</h3>
        <p>
          All thirteen lessons run in <code>bun run ci</code>. Tutorials rot when the product
          moves — ours already had, and its own header disagreed with the README about
          whether it had ever been executed. That is what this replaces.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <pre class="code">{@html sample.CI}</pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── What it found ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="found">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">What it found</p>
    <h2>Six defects, and every one of them exited 0.</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:64ch">
      Writing a tutorial that asserts is the first time anybody walks the whole path as a
      stranger would. These came out of doing it, and they are the argument for probing
      rather than trusting a status code: a tutorial graded on exit codes would have passed
      against all six and taught each of them.
    </p>

    <table class="table">
      <thead><tr><th>Id</th><th>What was wrong</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {#each found as f}
          <tr><td><code>{f.id}</code></td><td>{f.what}</td></tr>
        {/each}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="lede" style="max-width:64ch">
      The last one is the one worth reading. <code>execSync</code> with
      <code>stdio: 'inherit'</code> ignores its <code>input</code> on Node and honors it on
      Bun — and every command a deploy sends travels on stdin. So under Node the container
      swap, the lock release and the nginx write all reported success and did nothing.
      Every defect is filed in the repo's own register with the measurement that found it.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── Start ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="start">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Start</p>
    <h2>One command, and nothing to clean up.</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:64ch">
      It builds in a throwaway directory by default and removes it when it is done. Pass
      <code>--workspace ~/somewhere</code> to keep what it makes, which is the point if you
      want to read the app afterwards — and to stop and come back to it, since running a
      lesson again picks up where you left off.
    </p>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:64ch">
      Every step says what it is about to do and waits for you. Press enter to go on, or
      <code>n</code> to stop — the step you stopped at is the one the next run starts from.
      <code>--yes</code> runs a lesson straight through, which is what CI passes.
    </p>
    <pre class="code">{@html sample.START}</pre>
    <div class="cluster">
      <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#code">See the code it writes</a>
      <a class="btn outlined" href="/flow/">Follow one field through it</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/#packages">The packages</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="container pagenote">
  <span>Every sample on this page is a transcript or a verbatim lift from the step that produced it.</span>
  <span><a href="/journey/">The journey →</a></span>
</div>

<style>
  section { padding: var(--space-3xl) 0; }
  section + section { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }

  .hero { display: grid; gap: var(--space-2xl); align-items: center; }
  @media (min-width: 60rem) { .hero { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }

  h1 { font-size: var(--text-4xl); line-height: var(--leading-display); letter-spacing: -.02em; margin: 0; }
  h2 { letter-spacing: -.01em; }

  .eyebrow {
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .08em;
    font-size: var(--text-2xs); color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0;
  }
  .lede { color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: var(--leading-relaxed); }
  .cap  { font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0 0 var(--space-2xs); }

  /* The mark that means "asked and answered". It is the page's one piece of
     color vocabulary and it is a TONE, never a hex. */
  .tick { color: var(--color-success); font-weight: 700; }

  .pair { display: grid; gap: var(--space-lg); }
  /* The right column is the longer of the two — a transcript is short and the
     source that produced it is not — so the split is uneven rather than even,
     which is what keeps the gap under the left one from reading as a mistake. */
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .pair { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .85fr) minmax(0, 1.15fr); align-items: start; }
  }
  .pair pre { margin: 0; }

  .note {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-primary);
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
    padding: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg);
    border-radius: var(--card-radius);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
  }

  .lessons { display: grid; gap: var(--space-lg); }
  @media (min-width: 52rem) { .lessons { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }

  .lesson { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-sm); }
  .lesson h3 { margin: 0; }
  .lesson p  { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: var(--leading-relaxed); }
  .cmd { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-primary); }

  .foot {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-sm); align-items: center;
    margin-top: auto; padding-top: var(--space-sm); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
  }
  .proof { color: var(--ink-mute); font-family: var(--font-mono); }

  .table code { font-size: var(--text-xs); }
</style>
